  <h2 id="why">Why Does Alcohol Make Me Feel Anxious the Next Day?</h2>
  <p>Alcohol is a depressant, which means it slows down processes in your brain and your central nervous system. It does so by increasing the volume of a neurotransmitter called gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), making you feel calm and uninhibited. At the same time, it decreases glutamate, slowing down your thoughts.</p>
  <p>These changes initially make you feel good while drinking. But, as you metabolize alcohol, your body has to work hard to restore the balance it had before you drank. You start to become more alert again, and this is when anxiety surfaces. Alcohol also dehydrates you, disrupts your sleep, and causes low blood sugar, which all contribute to your depleted state and anxiety.</p>
  <p>Anxiety after drinking alcohol can include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Faster heartbeat</li>
    <li>Shortness of breath</li>
    <li>Agitation</li>
    <li>Sweating</li>
    <li>Problems concentrating</li>
    <li>Feeling regret, shame, or sadness</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Among people who drink socially and who don't meet criteria for alcohol use disorder (AUD), 22% experience hangxiety symptoms.</p>

