  <h2 id="stop">How Can I Stop Alcohol-Induced Anxiety?</h2>
  <p>The only way to stop anxiety after drinking alcohol is to not drink alcohol. For people who consume alcohol casually, have no dependency on alcohol or any mental health disorders, stopping abruptly may be a safe option.</p>
  <p>Anyone with AUD or a co-occurring mental health disorder should stop drinking only under medical supervision, since withdrawal can be dangerous without it. Supervised <a href="/programs/medical-detox/">detox programs</a> ensure that you are safe, monitored, and gradually taper off alcohol at a pace that supports your physical and mental wellbeing. It protects you from potentially life-threatening withdrawal symptoms such as delirium tremens (DT) and seizures, plus lowers the risk of relapse, contributing to longer-term recovery.</p>
  <p>Stopping or slowly reducing your alcohol intake allows neurotransmitters to regulate and restore your brain's chemical balance. This recalibration lowers anxiety, as well as leads to better sleep and mood.</p>
  <p>It's equally beneficial to address the underlying causes of anxiety. Whether you have general anxiety or an anxiety disorder, without alcohol as a veil over anxiety, you can more effectively explore triggers and treatment for your feelings and responses.</p>
  <p>In a study of people in treatment for AUD, 45% said mental health reasons, including anxiety, drove their decision to quit alcohol.</p>

  <h2 id="factors">What Factors Worsen Anxiety After Drinking Alcohol?</h2>
  <p>Being aware of the environmental and biological factors that exacerbate anxiety after drinking can help lower it. Keep in mind that adjusting these factors (where possible) won't completely prevent or stop anxiety.</p>
  <p>These factors apply to people with and without preexisting mental health disorders:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Alcohol quantity.</b> Higher volumes of alcohol make hangxiety worse.</li>
    <li><b>Drinking often.</b> Frequent alcohol consumption makes it harder for the body to rebalance afterward.</li>
    <li><b>Mental health disorders and mood disorders.</b> People with these disorders are more likely to feel anxiety, and to a greater degree, after drinking.</li>
    <li><b>Stress.</b> Alcohol triggers the release of cortisol (the stress hormone), which can make existing stress worse.</li>
    <li><b>Older age.</b> Aging slows the metabolism, meaning hangovers tend to be more intense and anxiety-filled.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="link">The Link Between Alcohol, Anxiety, and Mental Health</h2>
  <p>Many people who suffer from addiction are also prone to anxiety. Around 80% of people with AUD report periods of sadness, with 1 in 3 people experiencing severe depression. At least 40% also reported one or more intense panic attacks.</p>
  <p>Often, people use alcohol to numb anxiety, but as the effects wear off, negative feelings come rushing back, usually seeming stronger than before. The problem is, as a person consumes more alcohol to ineffectively deal with anxiety, the more they build a tolerance to its depressant effects, which only leads to more drinking as they chase the sense of escape — and eventually more anxiety.</p>
  <p>Research shows that AUD is an increased risk for people with anxiety or mood disorders. Meanwhile, 20-40% of people with a mental health disorder already have AUD.</p>
  <p>Stopping alcohol-induced anxiety calls for a closer look at mental health and damaging behaviors around alcohol consumption. The two are both detrimental and reinforcing of one another. By treating them together, and identifying the circumstances that fuel them, anxiety, addiction, and deeper challenges can heal. That's what <a href="/programs/dual-diagnosis-treatment/">dual diagnosis treatment</a> is designed for.</p>

